## 🧪 Testing

The following test cases can be used to check the website:

| Test Case | Action                      | Expected Result                                      |
| --------- | --------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
| 1         | Open `index.html`           | Dashboard should appear                              |
| 2         | Click Run Pipeline          | Pipeline execution should begin                      |
| 3         | Observe the stages          | Each stage should change to Running and then Success |
| 4         | Observe the logs            | Execution messages should appear                     |
| 5         | Run the pipeline again      | A new entry should appear in the history             |
| 6         | Click Reset after execution | Pipeline stages should return to Pending             |

## 🔧 DevOps Integration

### Git and GitHub

Git is used to track project changes, while GitHub stores the source code online.

The project can be updated through multiple meaningful commits to demonstrate version control.

### Jenkins

Jenkins can be integrated to automate project checks.

A Jenkins pipeline can:

* Retrieve the latest code from GitHub.
* Check whether the required files exist.
* Validate JavaScript syntax.
* Display the build result.

A successful Jenkins build demonstrates an actual automated workflow, separate from the browser-based pipeline simulation.

## 🚀 Future Enhancements

* Add simulated pipeline failures.
* Display pipeline execution duration.
* Add more pipeline stages.
* Store pipeline history using browser LocalStorage.
* Integrate Jenkins for automated checks.
* Add a real deployment workflow.

## ✅ Advantages

* Easy to understand and use.
* Suitable for beginners.
* Requires only HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for the simulator.
* Demonstrates important DevOps concepts visually.
* Can be extended with GitHub and Jenkins.

## 📝 Conclusion

The CI/CD Pipeline Simulator is a beginner-friendly educational project that demonstrates the basic stages of a CI/CD pipeline through an interactive website.

It helps students understand pipeline execution, status monitoring, and the role of automation in DevOps. The project can be further enhanced with GitHub and Jenkins to demonstrate real automated checks.

